---
import "../styles/ram.css";

import RamJourney from "./RamJourney.jsx";

import switchPokemon from "../assets/switch-pokemon.png";
import gameboy from "../assets/gameboy.png";
import nintendoSwitch from "../assets/ir-nintendo.png";

import arrowsGraphic from "../assets/arrows.png";
import pointLineGraphic from "../assets/point_line.png";
import titleLineGraphic from "../assets/title_line.png";
import topCornerGraphic from "../assets/top_corner.png";
import pointerGraphic from "../assets/pointer.png";
import cubeQuestion from "../assets/cube.png";

import EvolutionLogic from "../components/Evolution.jsx"
import MemComEvolution from "../components/MemoryCommunication.jsx"
---

<section class="ram-stage">
  <div class="ram-page">

    <header id="title" class="ram-hero">
      <div class="hero-circle-glow"></div>

      <img class="hud-graphic hud-top-corner" src={topCornerGraphic.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
      <img class="hud-graphic hud-title-line" src={titleLineGraphic.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
      <img class="hud-graphic hud-point-line" src={pointLineGraphic.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />

      <div class="discussion-label">
        <span class="label-title">DISCUSSION POINTS</span>
      </div>

      <img class="hud-graphic hud-arrows" src={arrowsGraphic.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />

      <div class="hook-text">
        Did you know? The first iteration of the RAM is a huge drum?
      </div>

      <div class="discussion-points">
        <a href="#what-is-ram">What is RAM?</a>
        <a href="#the-evolution-of-ram">The Evolution of RAM</a>
        <a href="#inside-the-ram-the-internal-data-journey">Inside RAM</a>
        <a href="#memory-access-and-communication">Memory Access and Communication</a>
      </div>

      <img class="hero-switch" src={switchPokemon.src} alt="Game memory visual" />

      <div class="facts-text">
        ARE RAMS TODAY REALLY THAT IMPORTANT?
      </div>

      <div class="more-text">
        DO DATA REALLY PERSIST WHEN TURNED OFF?
      </div>

      <div class="hero-title-block">
        <h1 class="glow-title">INSIDE RAM</h1>
        <p>COMPUTER MEMORY IN MOTION</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section id="what-is-ram" class="what-section">
      <div class="what-left">
        <h3>Did you know?</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The evolution of RAM has come a long way from being bulky to being small</li>
          <li>One example of the RAM becoming small is with the handheld console Nintendo Game Boy
          <li>Its processing is limited to only ~4.19 MHz, but enables the console to hide its internal parts and become portable</li>
          <li>The Game Paks were also limited in memory size, being only 32 KB</li>
          <li>Decades later, these limitations became obsolete, with the Nintendo Switch 2 having a custom processor from NVIDIA and a 256 GB storage</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="what-callout">
          <ul>
            <li>Main memory: Sharp SM83</li>
            <ul>
              <li>Hybrid of Zilog Z80 and Intel 8080</li>
              <li>Runs at ~4.19 MHz</li>
            </ul>
            <li>Game PAK</li>
            <ul>
              <li>32 KB ROM</li>
              <li>Extra RAM for saving states</li>
            </ul>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <img class="pointer" src={pointerGraphic.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
      </div>

      <img class="gameboy-image" src={gameboy.src} alt="Game Boy memory visual" />

      <div class="what-right">
        <h2 class="glow-title">WHAT IS RAM?</h2>
        <p>COMPUTER MEMORY IN MOTION</p>

        <div class="ram-fundamentals">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Working Memory:</strong> High-speed workspace used by the CPU to store actively running programs and temporary binary data. It allows the processor to fetch data near-instantly.</li>
            <li><strong>Random Access:</strong> Unlike sequential storage, the CPU uses explicit memory addresses to access any cell directly and instantly. This keeps retrieval times identical across the entire board.</li>
            <li><strong>Volatility:</strong> RAM requires constant power; its entire payload vanishes instantly when the system is turned off. It serves strictly as a live, temporary canvas.</li>
            <li><strong>RAM vs. ROM:</strong> RAM is dynamic (Read/Write) for active data, while ROM is permanent (Read-Only) for unchangeable boot firmware. ROM retains critical code safely even without power.</li>
            <li><strong>Bus Communication:</strong> CPU interacts with memory via Address (location), Data (payload), and Control (read/write signals) buses. These physical paths move data smoothly on every cycle.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="the-evolution-of-ram" class="big-section">
      <h2 class="glow-title">THE EVOLUTION OF RAM</h2>
      <p class="title-caption">COMPUTER MEMORY THROUGH TIME</p>

      <div class="evolution-upper">
        <div class="evolution-sides">
          <h2 class="evolution-name">DDR SDRAM</h2>
          <p>Early 2000s</p>

          <ul class="evolution-features">
            <li>
              <strong>Feature 1:</strong> desc
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Feature 2:</strong> desc
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Feature 3:</strong> desc
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="test-box">
          <img class="dram-image" alt="DRAM visual"/>
        </div>
        <div class="evolution-sides">
          <h3>Description</h3>

          <p>lorep ipsum bla bla scoobydoodoo i just hit the jackpot</p>
          <p>lorep ipsum bla bla scoobydoodoo i just hit the jackpot</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="evolution-lower">
        <button class="scroll-left">◀</button>
          <div class="scroll-section">
            <div class="evolution-button">1</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">2</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">3</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">4</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">5</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">6</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">7</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">8</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">9</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">10</div>
            <div class="evolution-button">11</div>            
            <div class="evolution-button">12</div>            
            <div class="evolution-button">13</div>            
          </div>
        <button class="scroll-right">▶</button>
      </div>

      <EvolutionLogic client:load />
    </section>

 <section id="inside-the-ram-the-internal-data-journey" class="big-section">
      <h2 class="glow-title title-absolute">INSIDE THE RAM</h2>
      <p class="title-caption caption-absolute">THE INTERNAL DATA JOURNEY</p>
      
      <div class="switch-container">
        <img class="nintendo-image" src={nintendoSwitch.src} alt="Nintendo Switch" />
        
        <div class="switch-screen-content">
          <RamJourney client:load />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="memory-access-and-communication" class="big-section">
      <!-- Header -->
    <div class="memory-header">
        <h2 class="glow-title">MEMORY ACCESS SIMULATOR</h2>
        <p class="memory-subtitle">
            CPU TO RAM COMMUNICATION
        </p>
    </div>
    <!-- Main Content -->
    <div class="memory-main">
        <!-- LEFT PANEL -->
        <aside class="memory-left">
            <div class="memory-card cpu-request">
                <h3>CPU REQUEST</h3>
                <div class="request-item">
                    <span>Operation</span>
                    <strong>READ</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="request-item">
                    <span>Address</span>
                    <strong>0x2B40</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="request-item">
                    <span>Data</span>
                    <strong>Pikachu</strong>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="memory-card how-it-works">
                <h3>HOW IT WORKS</h3>
                <p>
                    The controller sends a row address (RAS) to open the memory line, then a column address (CAS) to read and transfer the data.
                </p>
            </div>
        </aside>

        <!-- CENTER PANEL -->
        <section class="memory-center">
            <div class="memory-grid-header">
                <span></span>
                {
                    Array.from({ length: 12 }).map((_, col) => (
                        <span>C{col}</span>
                    ))
                }
            </div>
            <div class="memory-grid">
                {
                    Array.from({ length: 5 }).map((_, row) => (
                        <>
                            <span class="row-label">
                                R{row}
                            </span>
                            {
                                Array.from({ length: 12 }).map((_, col) => (
                                    <div
                                        class={`memory-cell
                                            ${row === 2 ? "active-row" : ""}
                                            ${col === 4 ? "active-column" : ""}
                                            ${
                                                row === 2 && col === 4
                                                    ? "selected-cell"
                                                    : ""
                                            }`}
                                    >
                                    </div>
                                ))
                            }
                        </>
                    ))
                }
            </div>
            <div class="memory-step">
                Click any cell to see how RAS (row) and CAS (column) addresses split to target data in the matrix. Watch the lines animate and highlight the exact intersection being transferred.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- RIGHT PANEL -->
        <aside class="memory-right">
            <div class="memory-card">
                <h3>CURRENT SELECTION</h3>
                <p><strong>Row: </strong> R2 <strong> Column:</strong> C4</p>
                <p><strong>Payload:</strong> Pikachu</p>
            </div>
            <div class="memory-card">
                <h3>SIGNAL ANALYZER</h3>
                <h4>RAS</h4>
                <p>
                    RAS latches the row address into DRAM before memory access
                    continues.
                </p>
                <h4>CAS</h4>
                <p>
                    CAS selects the target column from the active row so the
                    requested data can be transferred.
                </p>
            </div>
        </aside>
    </div>

    <!-- Bottom Section -->
    <MemComEvolution client:load />
    </section>

    <section id="challenge-quiz" class="big-section quiz-section">
      <h2 class="glow-title">CHALLENGE QUIZ</h2>
      <p class="title-caption">TEST YOUR KNOWLEDGE</p>

      <div class="quiz-container">
        <div class="quiz-left">
          <h3 class="question-number">QUESTION 1</h3>
          <p class="question-text">
            This section will reinforce the viewer's learning by asking
            questions about the information presented in the previous sections
          </p>

          <div class="cube-container">
            <div class="cube-glow-bg"></div>
            <img src={cubeQuestion.src} alt="Glowing Question Cube" class="quiz-cube" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-right">
          <button class="quiz-option">
            <span class="option-letter">A.</span>
            <span class="option-text">The answer in sentence form</span>
          </button>
          <button class="quiz-option">
            <span class="option-letter">B.</span>
            <span class="option-text">The answer in sentence form</span>
          </button>
          <button class="quiz-option">
            <span class="option-letter">C.</span>
            <span class="option-text">The answer in sentence form</span>
          </button>
          <button class="quiz-option">
            <span class="option-letter">D.</span>
            <span class="option-text">The answer in sentence form</span>
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <script>
      const quizOptions = document.querySelectorAll('.quiz-option')

      quizOptions.forEach(option => {
        option.addEventListener('click', () => {
          // Remove active class from all options
          quizOptions.forEach(opt => opt.classList.remove('active'));

          // Add active class to the clicked option
          option.classList.add('active')
        });
      });

    </script>

  </div>
</section>

